= A6
{c}
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= a
{parent=A6}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Both in-plane orientations minimize the one-site <energy>. Since every <adsorption site> is occupied, the sites label independent choices even though the molecules are chemically identical. The <ground state> of the whole system therefore has
$$
\boxed{U_{\min}=-N\varepsilon,\qquad\Omega_0=2^N.}
$$
The <ground-state degeneracy> is extensive: independently choosing either in-plane orientation at each site costs no <energy>. There is no extra vacancy or free-translational contribution in this model.

= b
{parent=A6}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Write $\beta=(k_{\rm B}T)^{-1}$ and $x=\beta\delta$. The one-site <partition function> includes both degenerate lower states,
$$
q=e^{\beta\varepsilon}(2+e^{-x}).
$$
The occupied <adsorption sites> are distinguishable positions, so the <canonical partition function> is a product, **without an additional $N!$ divisor**:
$$
\boxed{Z_N=q^N=e^{\beta N\varepsilon}(2+e^{-\beta\delta})^N.}
$$
Differentiating $\ln Z_N$ gives the <internal energy>,
$$
\boxed{U=-\left(\frac{\partial\ln Z_N}{\partial\beta}\right)_N
=-N\varepsilon+\frac{N\delta e^{-\beta\delta}}{2+e^{-\beta\delta}}
=-N\varepsilon+\frac{N\delta}{1+2e^{\beta\delta}}.}
$$
The final factor is the population of the perpendicular orientation. The shared binding offset $-\varepsilon$ shifts all <energy levels> equally and does not determine their relative populations.

= c
{parent=A6}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

At low <temperature>, the upper orientation has negligible <Boltzmann weight>, whereas at high <temperature> all three orientations become equally probable. Consequently
$$
\boxed{U(0)=-N\varepsilon,\qquad U(\infty)=-N\varepsilon+\frac{N\delta}{3}.}
$$
The high-temperature limit is a finite arithmetic mean of three available one-site <energy levels>. It is not an unbounded translational or rotational <equipartition> contribution: the prescribed site model has only three states per molecule. Their common binding offset can keep this limiting <internal energy> negative without any inconsistency.

= d
{parent=A6}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

For $k_{\rm B}T\gg\delta$, the <Boltzmann factors> of the orientations become almost equal, giving $3^N$ nearly equiprobable <microstates>. The <Boltzmann entropy> of that distribution approaches
$$
\boxed{S_{\max}=Nk_{\rm B}\ln3.}
$$
**The exact limiting value is reached only as $T\to\infty$** for a nonzero splitting. The useful crossover scale is $T\sim\delta/k_{\rm B}$; one needs $T\gg\delta/k_{\rm B}$ for a good saturation approximation. The binding offset $\varepsilon$ is not an additional population scale here because all sites remain occupied and it shifts every orientation equally.

= e
{parent=A6}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=e}

Using $dx/dT=-x/T$ in the <internal energy> gives
$$
\boxed{C_V=Nk_{\rm B}\frac{2x^2e^{-x}}{(2+e^{-x})^2},\qquad x=\frac{\delta}{k_{\rm B}T}.}
$$
This is a <Schottky anomaly with unequal degeneracies>, with lower-level <state degeneracy> two and upper-level <state degeneracy> one. Its limiting forms are
$$
\boxed{C_V\sim\frac{Nk_{\rm B}}2x^2e^{-x}\quad(T\to0),\qquad
C_V\sim\frac{2Nk_{\rm B}}9x^2\quad(T\to\infty).}
$$
It vanishes at low <temperature> because exciting the upper orientation is exponentially unlikely. It also vanishes at high <temperature> because the populations have nearly saturated and further heating hardly changes the <internal energy>. Between these limits it has one smooth maximum. The original plot shows its shape; numerical maximization gives $k_{\rm B}T_{\max}/\delta\simeq0.449$ and $C_{\max}/(Nk_{\rm B})\simeq0.241$.

\Image[paper-2-orientation-thermodynamics.png]
{title=Original heat-capacity peak and entropy limits for two lower orientations and one excited orientation}

= f
{parent=A6}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=f}

Only the two lower orientations remain populated, giving $2^N$ equiprobable <microstates>. Thus
$$
\boxed{\lim_{T\to0}S=Nk_{\rm B}\ln2.}
$$
This <residual entropy> follows from the stipulated independent-site <ground-state degeneracy>. The zero-entropy version of the <third law of thermodynamics> presupposes a unique ordered <ground state>; that assumption does not hold here. Intermolecular interactions or a small splitting of the two in-plane states could remove this degeneracy, but they are excluded from the given model.

= A7
{c}
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= a
{parent=A7}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Let $\Lambda=h/\sqrt{2\pi mk_{\rm B}T}$ be the <thermal de Broglie wavelength>. The two free momentum components of one adsorbed atom give the phase-space integral
$$
q_{\rm trans}^{(2)}=\frac1{h^2}\int_{\sigma}d^2r\int_{\mathbb R^2}dp_x\,dp_y\,
e^{-\beta(p_x^2+p_y^2)/(2m)}
=\frac{\sigma}{h^2}(2\pi mk_{\rm B}T).
$$
Each momentum integral is a <Gaussian integral>, so
$$
\boxed{q_{\rm trans}^{(2)}=\frac{\sigma}{\Lambda^2},\qquad
Z_N^{(2)}=\frac1{N!}\left(\frac{\sigma}{\Lambda^2}\right)^N.}
$$
The second expression is the <classical ideal-gas partition function> for mobile, indistinguishable surface atoms. Its validity requires a dilute nondegenerate gas, $N\Lambda^2/\sigma\ll1$, and neglects interactions and excited motion normal to the surface. The $N!$ appears here because particles move between positions, unlike the fully occupied fixed-site model of A6.

= b
{parent=A7}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

For the same atoms in a three-dimensional volume $V$, the one-atom <translational partition function> is $q_{\rm trans}^{(3)}=V/\Lambda^3$. Comparing the same $N$ atoms at the same <temperature> cancels the common $N!$ in their <canonical partition functions>. Therefore the molar <adsorption confinement free energy>, with the sign defined as adsorbed minus free, is
$$
\boxed{\Delta F_m^{\rm trans}=-RT\ln\frac{q_{\rm trans}^{(2)}}{q_{\rm trans}^{(3)}}
=RT\ln\frac{V}{\sigma\Lambda}.}
$$
The logarithm is dimensionless: $V/\sigma$ and $\Lambda$ are both lengths. For a macroscopic gas depth $V/\sigma\gg\Lambda$, confinement **raises the translational <Helmholtz free energy>**. Equivalently, the translational state count is reduced by a factor $\sigma\Lambda/V$ per atom. The question supplies neither a volume nor an adsorption binding <energy>, so this answer is symbolic and describes the confinement cost, not a numerical net adsorption free <energy>.

For example, a binding <energy> $D>0$ per atom would supply a separate $-N_{\rm A}D$ to $\Delta F_m$, together with any normal-motion internal contribution retained by a more detailed model. Adsorption can then be favourable despite the loss of free translational phase space. Merely calling the change a loss must not reverse the sign of the explicitly defined $F_{\rm ads}-F_{\rm gas}$.

= c
{parent=A7}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

A <planar rigid rotor> has angular eigenfunctions $e^{im\phi}$ and single-valuedness imposes $m=0,\pm1,\pm2,\ldots$. Its <energy> depends on $m^2$, so if the level is labelled by $J=|m|$, then
$$
\boxed{J=0,1,2,\ldots;\qquad g_0=1,\quad g_J=2\ (J\ge1).}
$$
The two states at $J>0$ represent opposite directions of <angular momentum>. If the signed <quantum number> itself is called $J$, each signed state is counted once instead; one must not both sum over both signs and multiply by two again.

For an unrestricted three-dimensional <linear rigid rotor>, $J=0,1,2,\ldots$ has $M=-J,\ldots,J$ and <state degeneracy> $2J+1$, with $E_J=\hbar^2J(J+1)/(2I)$. Planar confinement removes one rotational coordinate and changes both the spectrum and degeneracy. These are the spatial counts before <nuclear spin statistics>. For homonuclear <hydrogen>, the allowed even/odd spatial states must also be paired with the appropriate <parahydrogen> or <orthohydrogen> nuclear states, as considered explicitly in A8.

= d
{parent=A7}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

Define $\theta_{\rm rot}=\hbar^2/(2Ik_{\rm B})$. Summing the spatial states counted in part (c),
$$
q_{\rm planar}^{\rm labelled}=\sum_{m=-\infty}^{\infty}e^{-m^2\theta_{\rm rot}/T}
=1+2\sum_{J=1}^{\infty}e^{-J^2\theta_{\rm rot}/T}.
$$
When $T\gg\theta_{\rm rot}$ the closely spaced terms can be replaced by a <Gaussian integral>:
$$
\boxed{q_{\rm planar}^{\rm labelled}\simeq\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}
e^{-m^2\theta_{\rm rot}/T}\,dm
=\sqrt{\frac{\pi T}{\theta_{\rm rot}}}
=\frac{\sqrt{8\pi^3Ik_{\rm B}T}}h.}
$$
Here labelled means that the two ends of the molecular axis were distinguished in the angular count. In the usual high-temperature molecular convention that removes equivalent orientations and factors out nuclear-spin multiplicity, divide by the <rotational symmetry number> $s=2$ for homonuclear <hydrogen>:
$$
\boxed{q_{\rm rot}^{\rm planar}\simeq\frac1s\sqrt{\frac{\pi T}{\theta_{\rm rot}}},\qquad s=2.}
$$
The full exact <spin isomers of hydrogen> count is instead the even-$m$ spatial sum plus three times the odd-$m$ sum; its high-temperature limit is $2\sqrt{\pi T/\theta_{\rm rot}}$, including the four nuclear-spin states. These conventions differ only by temperature-independent factors in this limit. All give $U_{\rm rot,m}=RT/2$ and $C_{V,m}^{\rm rot}=R/2$, consistent with one quadratic rotational degree of freedom. By comparison, the free <linear rigid rotor> has $q_{\rm rot}\propto T$ and $C_{V,m}^{\rm rot}=R$.

= A8
{c}
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= a
{parent=A8}
{scope}

= i
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

The two <protons> are identical spin-one-half <fermions>, so the total state is antisymmetric under exchange. In the electronic and vibrational <ground state>, an even rotational $J$ has a symmetric spatial wavefunction and therefore uses the antisymmetric nuclear-spin <singlet state>; odd $J$ uses the symmetric <triplet state>. Thus <parahydrogen> has one nuclear-spin state, and <orthohydrogen> has three. Each rotational level also has spatial <state degeneracy> $2J+1$.

The <translational partition functions> and all common electronic/vibrational factors cancel in the ratio of equilibrium populations. Using the same zero of <energy> for the two <spin> species gives
$$
\boxed{K=\frac{n_o}{n_p}=\frac{q_o}{q_p}
=\frac{3\sum_{J\ {\rm odd}}(2J+1)e^{-J(J+1)\theta_{\rm rot}/T}}
{\sum_{J\ {\rm even}}(2J+1)e^{-J(J+1)\theta_{\rm rot}/T}}.}
$$
Subtracting the $J=1$ <energy> separately from all odd levels without restoring that offset in the equilibrium ratio would incorrectly remove the low-temperature preference for <parahydrogen>. The <nuclear spin statistics> account for the factor three, independently of the spatial factor $2J+1$.

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

At low <temperature>, retain $J=0$ in the even sum and $J=1$ in the odd sum. Their weights are respectively $1$ and $3e^{-2\theta_{\rm rot}/T}$ before the nuclear-spin multiplicity, so
$$
\boxed{K\simeq9e^{-2\theta_{\rm rot}/T}\longrightarrow0\qquad(T\ll\theta_{\rm rot}).}
$$
Equilibrium <hydrogen> then becomes almost entirely <parahydrogen>. At high <temperature>, many even and odd rotational levels contribute, and their spatial sums have the same leading value. Hence
$$
\boxed{K\longrightarrow3,\qquad x_o\longrightarrow\frac34,\quad x_p\longrightarrow\frac14
\qquad(T\gg\theta_{\rm rot}).}
$$
This is <normal hydrogen>. Its familiar high-temperature ratio is a nuclear-spin multiplicity ratio, not the $9:1$ ratio of the two lowest levels. Maintaining conversion is essential: a sample cooled without an effective conversion mechanism can retain its earlier composition instead of following $K(T)$. NBS thermodynamic data distinguish equilibrium composition from <normal hydrogen> and compare the state-sum prediction with spectroscopic and thermal evidence.<Compilation of Thermal Properties of Hydrogen in Its Various Isotopic and Ortho-Para Modifications by Harold W. Woolley and others>[Section III.2, printed pp. 394–395 and table 12: ortho–para equilibrium and its experimental checks]{ref}

= b
{parent=A8}
{scope}

= i
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

For a fixed number of non-interacting molecules, $Z_N=q^N/N!$ in the dilute <ideal gas> limit. The temperature-independent factorial drops out of the <internal energy> derivative:
$$
U_m=RT^2\left(\frac{\partial\ln q}{\partial T}\right)_V.
$$
Differentiate once more at fixed volume to obtain
$$
\boxed{\frac{C_{V,m}}R=2T\frac{q'}q
+T^2\left[\frac{q''}q-\left(\frac{q'}q\right)^2\right]
=\frac{d}{dT}\left(T^2\frac{d\ln q}{dT}\right).}
$$
Primes denote fixed-volume derivatives. If $q$ factorizes into translational, rotational and other independent contributions, their <heat capacities> add. In particular, the three-dimensional translational factor contributes $3R/2$, while a constant <spin degeneracy> factor contributes zero. The formula also follows from the positive <canonical energy variance>, $C_V=\operatorname{Var}(E)/(k_{\rm B}T^2)$.

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

For a high-temperature <linear rigid rotor>, $q_{\rm rot}=T/\theta_{\rm rot}$ up to temperature-independent symmetry/<spin> factors. Therefore $q'/q=1/T$ and $q''=0$, giving
$$
\boxed{\frac{C_{V,m}^{\rm rot}}R=0-1+2=1.}
$$
This agrees with the <equipartition theorem> for two quadratic rotational coordinates. It is the rotational contribution; adding free three-dimensional translation gives $C_{V,m}=5R/2$ when vibrations remain unexcited. A constant <rotational symmetry number> does not affect this derivative.

= c
{parent=A8}
{scope}

= i
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Apply part (b) to each supplied rotational sum. Multiplying the odd sum by its constant nuclear-spin factor three does not change $q'/q$ or $q''/q$. At the specified <temperature> the dimensionless rotational contributions are
$$
\begin{aligned}
\frac{C_{V,m,p}^{\rm rot}}R
&=\frac{22500(3.58\times10^{-5})}{1.165}
-22500\left(\frac{3.76\times10^{-3}}{1.165}\right)^2
+\frac{300(3.76\times10^{-3})}{1.165}
\simeq1.4253,\\
\frac{C_{V,m,o}^{\rm rot}}R
&=\frac{22500(-3.08\times10^{-5})}{0.970}
-22500\left(\frac{7.64\times10^{-3}}{0.970}\right)^2
+\frac{300(7.64\times10^{-3})}{0.970}
\simeq0.25265.
\end{aligned}
$$
Thus the **rotational <heat capacities> are $1.425R$ for <parahydrogen> and $0.253R$ for <orthohydrogen>**. For the total gas <heat capacities>, including translation and neglecting vibration,
$$
\boxed{C_{V,m,p}\simeq2.925R,\qquad C_{V,m,o}\simeq1.753R.}
$$
The difference is substantial because the permitted rotational ladders differ. The para ladder starts at $J=0$ and next reaches $J=2$, separated by $6k_{\rm B}\theta_{\rm rot}$; its excited population is changing appreciably here. The ortho ladder starts at $J=1$ and next reaches $J=3$, a larger separation of $10k_{\rm B}\theta_{\rm rot}$, so much of the ortho population remains in its lowest rotational manifold. A rotational contribution greater than $R$ is possible in the intermediate quantum regime and is not a violation of high-temperature <equipartition>.

= ii
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

For a <frozen ortho-para mixture>, the mole fractions do not change during the heat measurement, and the molar <heat capacity> is the composition-weighted average. A high-temperature composition retained on cooling has $x_o=3/4$, $x_p=1/4$, so
$$
\boxed{\frac{C_{V,m}^{\rm rot}}R=\tfrac14(1.425284)+\tfrac34(0.252645)
\simeq0.54581,\qquad C_{V,m}\simeq2.04581R.}
$$
For a mixture that **continues to interconvert and remain in equilibrium** near the specified <temperature>, use the combined rotational sum and differentiate that sum:
$$
q_{\rm eq}=q_{\rm even}+3q_{\rm odd}=4.075,\qquad
q_{\rm eq}'=0.02668\ {\rm K}^{-1},\qquad
q_{\rm eq}''=-5.66\times10^{-5}\ {\rm K}^{-2}.
$$
Consequently
$$
\boxed{\frac{C_{V,m,\rm eq}^{\rm rot}}R
=\frac{22500(-5.66\times10^{-5})}{4.075}
-22500\left(\frac{0.02668}{4.075}\right)^2
+\frac{300(0.02668)}{4.075}
\simeq0.68716,\qquad C_{V,m,\rm eq}\simeq2.18716R.}
$$
The equilibrium composition itself is $K=3(0.970)/1.165\simeq2.4979$, giving $x_o\simeq0.71411$. Its changing composition supplies a further positive <heat capacity>: warming converts some lower-energy para molecules into higher-energy ortho molecules. One can isolate this term as
$$
\frac{C_{V,m,\rm eq}^{\rm rot}}R
=x_p\frac{C_{V,m,p}^{\rm rot}}R+x_o\frac{C_{V,m,o}^{\rm rot}}R
+T^2x_px_o\left(\frac{q_{\rm odd}'}{q_{\rm odd}}-\frac{q_{\rm even}'}{q_{\rm even}}\right)^2.
$$
For clarity, if a mixture is first equilibrated at this <temperature> and then its composition is frozen during measurement, the conversion term is absent: its rotational value is $0.58789R$ and its total value is $2.08789R$. This is distinct from the <equilibrium heat capacity of interconverting states> even though the instantaneous composition is the same. An experiment must specify conversion activity and equilibration time; compare sufficiently slow <temperature> steps with a conversion catalyst against a composition-preserving sample, while keeping the same thermal background and checking that the sample has reached the stated <temperature>.

\Image[paper-2-hydrogen-heat-capacity.png]
{title=Original ideal-rotor heat capacities showing the difference between frozen normal hydrogen and continuously equilibrating hydrogen}

= A9
{c}
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= a
{parent=A9}
{scope}

= i
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

For the <free electron model>, the **<Fermi energy> is the highest occupied one-electron <energy> at zero <temperature>**, measured relative to the bottom of the free-electron band. The occupied <wavevectors> fill a disk in two dimensions or a sphere in three dimensions. The radius is the **<Fermi wavevector> $k_F$**, with
$$
\boxed{E_F=\frac{\hbar^2k_F^2}{2m_e}.}
$$
Each allowed <wavevector> has two <spin> states. At nonzero <temperature>, the <Fermi-Dirac distribution> rounds the occupation boundary; the zero-temperature <Fermi energy> remains a useful density-defined scale and should not be defined as the thermal <energy> $k_{\rm B}T$.

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Metallic atomic densities place very many <electrons> in a small volume. The <Pauli exclusion principle> prevents all these <fermions> occupying the lowest spatial state, so filling the available states produces a <Fermi energy> of order electronvolts. At room <temperature>, $k_{\rm B}T\simeq0.026\ {\rm eV}$, much smaller than that scale. Equivalently, **ordinary metals are at $T\ll T_F=E_F/k_{\rm B}$**, where $T_F$ is the <Fermi temperature>. Their <electrons> are quantum degenerate even at ordinary laboratory temperatures.

= iii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

Let $\mathcal A$ be the total two-dimensional area. There are $\mathcal A/(2\pi)^2$ <wavevector> states per reciprocal-space area per <spin>. Including <spin degeneracy> two and filling a disk gives
$$
N_e=2\frac{\mathcal A}{(2\pi)^2}\pi k_F^2
=\frac{\mathcal A k_F^2}{2\pi}.
$$
For areal <number density> $n_e=N_e/\mathcal A$ this yields
$$
\boxed{k_F=\sqrt{2\pi n_e},\qquad E_F=\frac{\pi\hbar^2 n_e}{m_e}.}
$$
The disk is centred at zero <wavevector>, and its boundary is a <fermi circle>. Omitting the two <spin> states would incorrectly double the <energy> inferred for a given <number density>.

= b
{parent=A9}
{scope}

= i
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Using $M_{\rm Au}\simeq196.97\ {\rm g\,mol^{-1}}$ and the <Avogadro constant>, the areal atom count is
$$
n_{\rm Au}=\frac{1.56\times10^{-6}\ {\rm g}}{196.97\ {\rm g\,mol^{-1}}}
\frac{6.02214\times10^{23}\ {\rm mol^{-1}}}{10^{-4}\ {\rm m^2}}
\simeq4.77\times10^{19}\ {\rm m^{-2}}.
$$
One atom occupies each square cell of area $a^2$, so the inferred <square lattice> spacing is
$$
\boxed{a=n_{\rm Au}^{-1/2}\simeq1.45\times10^{-10}\ {\rm m}=0.145\ {\rm nm}.}
$$
This uses the given deposited <mass> and the assumed single square layer; it does not substitute a bulk <gold> lattice parameter for the measured areal count.

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

One mobile <electron> per <gold> atom gives
$$
\boxed{n_e=n_{\rm Au}\simeq4.77\times10^{19}\ {\rm electrons\,m^{-2}}.}
$$
This is an areal <number density>; no layer thickness is required and it should not be converted into a volume density for the two-dimensional model.

= iii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

Substitution in part (a) gives
$$
\boxed{k_F=\sqrt{2\pi(4.77\times10^{19})}\simeq1.73\times10^{10}\ {\rm m^{-1}},}
$$
and
$$
\boxed{E_F=\frac{\pi\hbar^2(4.77\times10^{19})}{m_e}
\simeq1.83\times10^{-18}\ {\rm J}\simeq11.4\ {\rm eV}.}
$$
The conversion uses $1\ {\rm eV}=1.60218\times10^{-19}\ {\rm J}$. These are the predictions of the stipulated two-dimensional <free electron model>, with the vacuum <electron mass> and one carrier per inferred square cell.

= c
{parent=A9}
{scope}

= i
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

The <reciprocal lattice> is generated by
$$
\boxed{\mathbf b_1=\frac{2\pi}{a}\hat{\mathbf x},\qquad
\mathbf b_2=\frac{2\pi}{a}\hat{\mathbf y},\qquad
\mathbf G_{hk}=h\mathbf b_1+k\mathbf b_2.}
$$
Its points are spaced by $2\pi/a$ along both reciprocal axes. Perpendicular bisectors between the origin and its four nearest reciprocal neighbours enclose the <first Brillouin zone>,
$$
\boxed{-\frac\pi a\le k_x\le\frac\pi a,\qquad
-\frac\pi a\le k_y\le\frac\pi a.}
$$
The original drawing labels both the reciprocal-lattice spacing and the nearer zone boundaries. Its shaded disk is the occupied region for one <electron> per real-space cell.

\Image[paper-2-fermi-circle.png]
{title=Original square reciprocal lattice, first Brillouin zone and half-filled spin-degenerate Fermi disk}

= ii
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

For one <electron> per square cell, $n_e=1/a^2$, so
$$
\boxed{\frac{k_F}{\pi/a}=\sqrt{\frac2\pi}\simeq0.7979<1.}
$$
Numerically, $\pi/a\simeq2.17\times10^{10}\ {\rm m^{-1}}$ exceeds $k_F\simeq1.73\times10^{10}\ {\rm m^{-1}}$, so the <fermi circle> lies entirely inside the <first Brillouin zone>.

The state-counting explanation is that a spin-degenerate band has two <electron> states per primitive cell. One <electron> per cell therefore occupies half of that band. In the circular free-electron approximation the occupied reciprocal-space area is
$$
\frac{\pi k_F^2}{(2\pi/a)^2}=\frac12.
$$
A disk of that area fits inside this square: its radius is less than the distance to an edge. Half filling alone is not a shape-independent theorem that every possible <Fermi surface> must avoid every zone boundary; the square geometry and circular free-electron dispersion make the statement true here.

= iii
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

An isolated spin-degenerate lowest <energy band> accommodates two <electrons> per atom when there is one atom per primitive cell. Starting from one per atom, the estimate for filling the band is therefore
$$
\boxed{\text{one additional electron per Au atom}.}
$$
This corresponds to an additional areal carrier count of about $4.77\times10^{19}\ {\rm m^{-2}}$. If a genuine <band gap> separates the filled first band from the next band, there are no partially occupied band states at low <temperature>, so ordinary metallic conduction is lost.

Simply touching an edge is insufficient. In the circular approximation first contact would occur at $k_F=\pi/a$, corresponding to $n_ea^2=\pi/2\simeq1.57$ <electrons> per cell; that is a partly filled band and need not be insulating. Near zone boundaries, the periodic potential reshapes the free-electron contours. The two-electron filling estimate assumes a global gap without overlapping bands, rather than inferring an insulator merely from one local avoided crossing.

= A10
{c}
{parent=Paper 2}
{scope}

= a
{parent=A10}
{scope}

= i
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Assume dilute nondegenerate <electrons> and <electron holes>, parabolic three-dimensional band edges with the stated scalar <band effective masses>, <spin> multiplicity two, one effective valley for each edge, and thermal equilibrium. Take the unchanged host <ground state> to have unit activity and neglect bound excitons. The <effective density of states> values are
$$
N_c=2\left(\frac{2\pi m_e^*k_{\rm B}T}{h^2}\right)^{3/2},\qquad
N_v=2\left(\frac{2\pi m_h^*k_{\rm B}T}{h^2}\right)^{3/2}.
$$
For a <Fermi level> $\mu$ between the band edges,
$$
n_e=N_c e^{-(E_c-\mu)/(k_{\rm B}T)},\qquad
n_h=N_v e^{-(\mu-E_v)/(k_{\rm B}T)}.
$$
The factors of $\mu$ cancel on multiplication. With $E_g=E_c-E_v$, the <semiconductor mass-action law> is
$$
\boxed{K_n\equiv n_en_h=N_cN_v e^{-E_g/(k_{\rm B}T)}
=4\left(\frac{2\pi k_{\rm B}T}{h^2}\right)^3(m_e^*m_h^*)^{3/2}
e^{-E_g/(k_{\rm B}T)}.}
$$
This density-form equilibrium constant has units $\mathrm{m^{-6}}$. A dimensionless thermodynamic constant uses a specified reference <number density> $n^\circ$, giving $K=K_n/(n^\circ)^2$. The dot representing the host <ground state> is not a freely translating gas species requiring a further gas partition function. For an <intrinsic semiconductor>, <charge neutrality> gives $n_e=n_h=n_i=\sqrt{K_n}$.

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

The equal supplied masses give equal <effective density of states>. At the specified <temperature>,
$$
N_c=N_v=2\left[\frac{2\pi(0.3m_e)k_{\rm B}(400\ {\rm K})}{h^2}\right]^{3/2}
\simeq6.35\times10^{24}\ {\rm m^{-3}}.
$$
Using the same <energy> units in the exponential,
$$
n_i=N_c e^{-E_g/(2k_{\rm B}T)}
\simeq(6.35\times10^{24})e^{-0.67/[2(8.61733\times10^{-5})(400)]}
\simeq3.82\times10^{20}\ {\rm m^{-3}}.
$$
For equal <electron> and hole mobilities, both carriers contribute to the intrinsic <electrical conductivity>:
$$
\boxed{\mu_e=\mu_h=\frac{\sigma}{2en_i}
\simeq\frac{48}{2(1.60218\times10^{-19})(3.82\times10^{20})}
\simeq0.392\ {\rm m^2\,V^{-1}\,s^{-1}}.}
$$
The factor two is essential. A dimensional check gives
$$
\frac{\mathrm{S\,m^{-1}}}{\mathrm{C\,m^{-3}}}
=\frac{\mathrm{A\,V^{-1}\,m^2}}{\mathrm C}
=\mathrm{m^2\,V^{-1}\,s^{-1}},
$$
using $\mathrm A=\mathrm{C\,s^{-1}}$.

= iii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

Substitutional <arsenic> has one more valence <electron> than <germanium>, so it acts as a <donor impurity>. Thermal ionization of its shallow bound state supplies mobile conduction-band <electrons> and leaves fixed positive donor charge. The resulting <n-type semiconductor> can have $n_e\gg n_i$ even when only a small fraction of host atoms are replaced. Its <electrical conductivity> then rises because the mobile carrier count increases; the opposite donor charge preserves macroscopic <charge neutrality>.

This argument assumes that the donors are substantially ionized at the measurement <temperature> and that the added impurity scattering does not overwhelm the carrier increase. The screened bound-donor picture and its departures from a simple hydrogenic model are described in institutional <semiconductor> spectroscopy notes.<Optical Properties of Solids by Mildred S. Dresselhaus>[Chapter 7, sections 7.2–7.3, printed pp. 76–79: hydrogenic impurities and limitations]{ref}

= iv
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iv}

Retain the inferred mobilities and assume one ionized donor per added <arsenic> atom, negligible compensation, the same <band gap>, and the nondegenerate <semiconductor mass-action law>. Relative to the intrinsic sample, a conductivity factor $f$ requires
$$
n_e+n_h=2fn_i,\qquad n_en_h=n_i^2.
$$
For complete donor ionization, <charge neutrality> gives $N_D=n_e-n_h$, so
$$
\boxed{N_D=2n_i\sqrt{f^2-1}\simeq2fn_i
\simeq7.64\times10^{23}\ {\rm m^{-3}}
=7.64\times10^{17}\ {\rm cm^{-3}}\quad(f=1000).}
$$
The host atom density, using $M_{\rm Ge}\simeq72.63\ {\rm g\,mol^{-1}}$, is
$$
n_{\rm Ge}=\frac{5.32\times10^6\ {\rm g\,m^{-3}}}{72.63\ {\rm g\,mol^{-1}}}N_{\rm A}
\simeq4.41\times10^{28}\ {\rm m^{-3}}.
$$
Thus the required atomic fraction is
$$
\boxed{\frac{N_D}{n_{\rm Ge}}\simeq1.73\times10^{-5}
\simeq17.3\ \text{atomic ppm},}
$$
approximately one donor per $5.8\times10^4$ host atoms. The <electron> density must rise to about $2000n_i$, not $1000n_i$, because the original intrinsic sample conducted through both <electrons> and holes. This is an estimate under unchanged mobility: impurity scattering, incomplete ionization, compensation and band-edge changes can alter it. Here $n_e/N_c\simeq0.12$, so the nondegenerate treatment is an approximation rather than an arbitrarily dilute limit.

= b
{parent=A10}
{scope}

= i
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Two derivatives of the free-electron dispersion give
$$
\frac{d^2E_k}{dk^2}=\frac{\hbar^2}{m_e},\qquad
\boxed{m_e^*=m_e.}
$$
The <band effective mass> becomes the ordinary <electron mass> when no periodic-potential modification of the quadratic <dispersion relation> is present.

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Differentiating the <tight binding> dispersion produced by the <Hückel method> gives
$$
\frac{dE_k}{dk}=-2\beta a\sin(ka),\qquad
\frac{d^2E_k}{dk^2}=-2\beta a^2\cos(ka).
$$
Hence
$$
\boxed{\frac1{m_e^*(k)}=-\frac{2\beta a^2}{\hbar^2}\cos(ka),\qquad
m_e^*(k)=-\frac{\hbar^2}{2\beta a^2\cos(ka)}.}
$$
Unlike a free particle, the <band effective mass> depends on <wavevector> and can change sign. At an inflection point $\cos(ka)=0$, the inverse <mass> vanishes and the curvature-based <mass> diverges; a quadratic approximation then ceases to describe the dispersion locally. For the usual $\beta<0$ convention, the bottom at $k=0$ has positive <mass> and the top at $k=\pi/a$ has negative <electron mass>. The constant $\alpha$ has no effect on curvature.

= iii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

In a weakly doped <n-type semiconductor>, the mobile <electrons> occupy states near the bottom of the <conduction band>. Their local band curvature is positive, so **their <band effective mass> is positive and approximately constant sufficiently near that minimum**. For the usual $\beta<0$ convention, expand about $k=0$:
$$
E_k\simeq\alpha+2\beta-\beta a^2k^2,
\qquad
\boxed{m_e^*\simeq\frac{\hbar^2}{2|\beta|a^2}>0.}
$$
Stronger overlap gives larger $|\beta|$, greater curvature and a smaller <mass> within this model. Positive $\beta$ simply places the minimum at the zone boundary and again gives a positive edge <mass>. A negative <electron mass> near a band maximum instead motivates the positive-mass <electron hole> description; it is not the usual conduction-edge situation for an <n-type semiconductor>. No numerical comparison with $m_e$ follows without values of $a$ and $\beta$.
